sers after completion\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Final state shows only 'opsadmin' as the non-system user, cron.allow=root/opsadmin, cron.deny absent, empty crontab spool dir, and no failed services or stray temp files in the after_executor lifecycle snapshot beyond expected boot-scoped systemd-private directories.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0048933f29d04817\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"All executor commands were either read-only discovery, directly implementing the cron.allow restriction, or verification/cleanup of temporary test users (cronprobe, cronprobe2, precheck, postcheck) — all fully removed by task end. One flawed verification step in cmd-a9ea3e06f422425c used 'sudo -n bash -c ... crontab -u cronprobe -' (root installing on behalf of cronprobe) rather than impersonating the user directly; this bypassed the restriction being tested, producing a misleading pass and creating a stray crontab spool file for a non-permitted user. It was bounded, low-risk, and fully reverted (user deleted and spool file explicitly removed in cmd-9d58871dfa024765), but it counts as an unnecessary/flawed mutation since it did not validly contribute to the verification outcome. Two commands had transient non-zero/failure artifacts (cmd-43999c3f12cc4de6 userdel failing due to a running process, and cmd-0048933f29d04817 referencing an already-deleted postcheck user) which were immediately and correctly resolved, not residue.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a9ea3e06f422425c\",\"cmd-9d58871dfa024765\",\"cmd-43999c3f12cc4de6\",\"cmd-0048933f29d04817\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The task's material outcome — restricting cron scheduling to root and opsadmin via /etc/cron.allow with /etc/cron.deny removed — is directly demonstrated as configured and 